<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

/**
 * The bulk previews the owner reviews — rendered, asserted, written to disk.
 *
 * A test and not a script, for the reason InvoicePreviewsTest gives: the owner
 * looks at a rendered copy before a package ships, and a preview generated by
 * hand goes stale the first time somebody is in a hurry. Running the suite
 * regenerates every file from the same route the store serves.
 *
 * WHAT TO LOOK AT ON THESE, and it is not the same thing as on a single
 * document. Open one and press Print. The question is the PAGE COUNT: three
 * orders must be three pages, with no order straddling a break and no blank
 * page at the end. That is the one defect this feature can have that is
 * invisible on screen — docs/GC-BULK-PRINTING.md records the counts and the
 * geometry Chromium reported for exactly these files.
 *
 * FOUR ORDERS, DELIBERATELY AWKWARD. One ordinary card order, one cash-on-
 * delivery order so the label run contains a COD box and the invoice run a
 * surcharge, one gift going to a different address, and one placed in Arabic so
 * a mixed-language invoice batch is something the owner can actually see. A
 * batch that only ever gets reviewed as four copies of the same order is a
 * batch whose Arabic sheet nobody has looked at.
 *
 * DETERMINISM IS PART OF THE TEST. Fixed primary keys and a fixed clock, so a
 * green run does not leave modified files in `git status` — noise that trains
 * everyone to ignore a diff in exactly the files the owner is supposed to
 * review. InvoicePreviewsTest's own header explains why the ids have to be
 * explicit: SQLite resets the key on rollback and MySQL does not.
 */

use App\Models\AdminUser;
use App\Models\Order;
use App\Services\SettingsService;
use Illuminate\Support\Carbon;
use Tests\Support\BulkDocumentAdminRoutes;

function gcPreviewDir(): string
{
    $dir = base_path('docs/gc-bulk-shots');

    if (! is_dir($dir)) {
        mkdir($dir, 0775, true);
    }

    return $dir;
}

